    "case_id": "413",
    "case_summary": "A 63-year-old man presented with an asymptomatic, new-onset eruptive facial lesion. His medical history was notable for chronic high-grade myelodysplastic syndrome and refractory anemia with excess blasts.\n\nPhysical examination revealed a 3×2–mm smooth, red, dome-shaped papule under the right lower eyelid.\n\n{'Skin Biopsy': 'A shave biopsy specimen demonstrated a diffuse atypical dermal infiltrate of monocytoid-appearing cells admixed with rare eosinophils, uniformly positive for CD207/langerin and negative for myeloperoxidase. BRAF-V600E variant status was identified through immunohistochemical studies and confirmed by polymerase chain reaction analysis.', 'Bone Marrow Biopsy': 'Prior bone marrow biopsy specimens showed hypercellular bone marrow with atypical erythropoiesis and 10% to 15% myeloblasts.', 'Molecular Analysis': 'Next-generation sequencing targeted extensive gene profiles in the bone marrow aspirate and paraffin-embedded skin biopsy specimens revealed identical pathogenic variations in the ASXL1, IDH2, and STAG2 genes.', 'Images': {'Figure 1A': 'Red papule under the right lower eyelid', 'Figure 1B': 'Atypical diffuse dermal monocytoid-appearing infiltrate admixed with eosinophils (hematoxylin-eosin; original magnification ×40)', 'Figure 1C': 'Immunohistochemical studies reveal strong positivity for langerin (original magnification ×20)'}}",
